\ReportEntry{Appendix A\quad Acquisition tests and planning}
\PageTitle{A1 / ACQUISITION DESIGN}{Monthly assignment\newline and the two denominators}{Six enrollment waves, four channel--need tests per wave, and fresh prospects throughout.}
Each month, each test randomly assigns 500 never-paid prospects to its focal campaign and 500 to control. All assigned nonbuyers remain in the acquisition rates. Acquisitions through every route count over exactly 31 days; the end date is exclusive. The 24 tests use \VAR{b_test_prospects} distinct people, with no reuse across waves or simultaneous tests. Their research sample and budget are separate from the operating panel.
\TableCaption{channel-buyers}{Latest completed wave: \VAR{b_latest_test_month}. T is treatment; C is control. Earlier waves appear in Appendix A3--A4.}
\VAR{channel_counts_table}
For unequal arm sizes, scale the control by $k=N_T/N_C$. Additional acquisitions are $B_T-kB_C$; additional spending is $S_T-kS_C$.
\[
\text{Credited CAC}=\frac{S_T}{B_T},\qquad
\text{iCAC}=\frac{S_T-kS_C}{B_T-kB_C}.
\]
\TableCaption{channel-cost}{Latest wave. Each treatment arm spends \$10,000 on media and \$1,000 on other incremental acquisition costs. Control focal spending is zero.}
\VAR{channel_cost_table}
\Section{What the holdout removes}
Background acquisitions can reflect prior brand exposure and other marketing. They are acquisitions without the focal campaign, not a company-wide estimate of marketing-free organic demand. Every wave uses contemporaneous controls. Channels have separate eligible audiences; these are not head-to-head channel assignments.
\Evidence{Sources: \Code{channel_holdouts.csv}, saved SQL \Code{channel_cac.csv} for all tests, and \Code{channel_cac_current.csv} for each cell's latest result. The cost interpretation follows \SourceCite{larsen2019}{Larsen (2019)} and \SourceCite{course2026}{Tian (2026)}, Topic 3.}

\NewPage
\PageTitle{A2 / ACQUISITION UNCERTAINTY}{Acquisition lift\newline and uncertainty in iCAC}{Technical appendix | The acquisition denominator is uncertain even when recorded spending is fixed.}
\Section{Acquisition holdout uncertainty}
\TableCaption{channel-uncertainty}{Latest completed wave: approximate 95\% intervals for each test, with recorded costs fixed.}
\VAR{channel_interval_table}
\Evidence{The acquisition-rate difference uses an unpooled Bernoulli standard error over all assigned prospects. Scale the interval to treatment size, then invert its positive range for iCAC. A lift interval crossing zero has no finite upper iCAC bound. These are acquisition-cost intervals; the value projection does not claim a joint lifetime-return confidence interval.}

\Section{Interpretation and scope}
Each comparison includes all assigned prospects and its own control. These are per-test intervals, not simultaneous coverage for 24 tests. Each wave has a fixed sample and analysis date; the program does not stop a wave early when a favorable result appears. The monthly series describes changing cost estimates without identifying why they changed.
\Section{The value assumption is separate}
The final planning table joins the latest completed test in each cell to the latest common operational cohort with at least six observed receipts: \VAR{b_month}. It assumes additional customers have that contribution value and the campaign does not change the value of customers who would acquire anyway. The July-cost comparison holds today's value benchmarks fixed; it does not reconstruct a decision made in July. No experimental contribution, retention or lifetime outcomes enter the model.
\Section{Incremental cash recovery}
The saved benchmark cash path is already net of historical CAC. Add that cost back to obtain cumulative contribution, then find its first crossing of current iCAC. Count monthly payments from one. This is an undiscounted expected-path calculation; first-year value is discounted. \Code{incremental-planning.csv} saves both recovery measures and the break-even value share. It does not refit a model or measure realized experimental payback.

