\NewPage
\PageTitle{A5 / CONTINUOUS TESTING}{A recurring acquisition\newline measurement and spending cycle}{Each wave closes on schedule; the next spending review uses the newly completed evidence.}
\TableCaption{holdout-schedule}{Enrollment starts on the first of each month and ends exactly 31 days later. September and November results therefore become available on the second of the following month.}
\VAR{b_holdout_schedule}
\Section{The monthly operating process}
Marketing defines each wave's eligible audience, campaign and spending level before assignment. Analytics preserves assignment and counts all first paid acquisitions through the end of the window. Once the window closes, the new iCAC and its interval replace the prior estimate for that cell. Unfinished waves never inform the current estimate.

At each spending review, Finance and Marketing pair current costs with the latest suitably mature contribution benchmarks. They choose a spending step and acceptable recovery period, then retain holdouts at the new level. If eligibility, price or campaign changes, document that change with the new test; the earlier iCAC does not establish the cost of unlimited scale.
\Section{Fund the measurement program explicitly}
The observed program spends \VAR{b_wave_budget} per wave and \VAR{b_program_budget} across six waves. These budgets are separate from the operating ledger. The company exhibits therefore cannot be read as total cash available after testing: they exclude both test spending and subsequent test-customer contribution. Finance must reconcile that broader cash position before committing further funds.
\Evidence{Monthly repetition is this case's operating design. Larsen's acquisition-incrementality argument motivates the holdout correction; it does not prescribe this cadence or these sample sizes.}

